      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "48a37552-72d5-57d6-bf01-f45327eef46d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Canals: Sandy and Beaver Canal, Beaver and Erie Canal, Pennsylvania and Ohio Canal\n- Places: Bolivar, Ohio, Glasgow, Pennsylvania, Ohio River, Lake Erie, Beaver Creek State Park, Beaver County, Lawrence County, Mercer County, Crawford County, Erie County, Pennsylvania\n- Systems: Ohio and Erie Canal, Pennsylvania Canal system\n- Concepts: canal connections, private funding, historic remnants\n\nFacts:\n- The Sandy and Beaver Canal ran 73 miles from the Ohio and Erie Canal at Bolivar, Ohio, to the Ohio River at Glasgow, Pennsylvania.\n- The Beaver and Erie Canal, also known as the Erie Extension Canal, was part of the Pennsylvania Canal system.\n- The Beaver and Erie Canal consisted of the Beaver Division, the Shenango Division, and the Conneaut Division.\n- The Beaver and Erie Canal ran 136 miles north to south near the western edge of Pennsylvania from the Ohio River to Lake Erie through Beaver, Lawrence, Mercer, Crawford, and Erie counties.\n- Historic remnants of the Sandy and Beaver Canal can be found throughout Beaver Creek State Park.\n- The Pennsylvania and Ohio Canal was unique because it connected the Ohio and Erie Canal in Ohio with the Beaver and Erie Canal in Pennsylvania and was funded by private interests.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6185e4bf-a27e-50a1-b68b-163677860ccb",
        "text": "Page: Sandy and Beaver Canal. Sentence: The Sandy and Beaver Canal ran 73 mi from the Ohio and Erie Canal at Bolivar, Ohio, to the Ohio River at Glasgow, Pennsylvania.\nPage: Beaver and Erie Canal. Sentence: The Beaver and Erie Canal, also known as the Erie Extension Canal, was part of the Pennsylvania Canal system and consisted of three sections: the Beaver Division, the Shenango Division, and the Conneaut Division.\nPage: Beaver and Erie Canal. Sentence: The canal ran 136 mi north–south near the western edge of the state from the Ohio River to Lake Erie through Beaver County, Lawrence County, Mercer County, Crawford County, and Erie County, Pennsylvania.\nPage: Beaver Creek State Park. Sentence: Historic remnants of the Sandy and Beaver Canal can be found throughout the park.\nPage: Pennsylvania and Ohio Canal. Sentence: It was unique in that it served to connect canals in two states (the Ohio and Erie Canal in Ohio and the Beaver and Erie Canal in Pennsylvania) and was funded by private interests.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
